// Data Objects 

- Data sets hold things called data objects 
    - This is essentially an entity (a row, record, entry) in your table 
    - These are described by certain attributes / characteristics 
    - It goes from rows -> objects -> columns (attributes)

// Attributes 

- An attribute (aka dimensions, features, variables) are data fields that represent a characteristic or feature of a data object

- Attributes can be represented by a certain *type* of data 
    - Nominal
    - Ordinal
    - Binary 
    - Numeric: quantitative 
        - Interval-scaled
        - Ratio-scaled 

// Types of Attributes 

- Nominal: categories, states, or labels / names 
    - Hair_Color = { Auburn, Black, Blond, Brown, Red, }
    - Marital Stats, Occupation, ID numbers, Zip codes 

- Binary 
    - This is a nominal attribute with only 2 states (0 and 1)
        - Symmetric binary: both outcomes are equally important (heads and tails) 
        - Asymmetric binary: outcomes are not equally important (medical test -> positive vs negative) 
            - For assymetric, you want to assign the binary 1 to the most important outcome 

- Ordinal
    - Values have a meaningful order but the magnitude between successive values is not known 
        - Size = { small, medium, large }, grades, army rankings 
    - Its structured, but the distance between each item isnt specific

// Numeric Attribute Types 

- Interval-Scaled 
    - Measured on a scale of equal-sized units 
    - The values have order 
        - Ex: temperature in Celsius or Fahrenheit, calendar dates 
        - No true zero-point 
        - You can tell which values are bigger/smaller, but you cant say something is twice as much as something else 

- Ratio-Scaled 
    - Inherent zero-point
    - Values are an order of magnitude larger than the unit of measurement (10 awards is twice as many as 5 awards) 
        - Ex: temperature in Kelvin, length, height, weight, monetary quantities 
    - This one you can compare using twice as much. $0 = no money -> $20 is twice $10. 

// Discrete vs. Continuous 

- Discrete 
    - Finite or countably infinite set of values 
        - Ex: zip codes, profession, number of words in a doc 
    - These are sometimes represented as int values 
    - Note: binary attributes are a special case of discrete attributes 

- Continuous 
    - Has real numbers as attribute values 
        - Ex: Temperature, height, or weight 
    - In practicality, real values can only be measured and represented using a finite number of digits 
    - Typically represented as a float 
        - Ex: 5.5, 0.0001, -2,345.6789

Not every Float is Continuous 
    - A shoe size (9, 9.5, 10, 10.5) is NOT continuous 
        - this is because theres no such shoe size as like a 9.102 
- You can put shoe sizes in a dropdown list.

// Basic Statistical Descriptions of Data 

- Motivation: to better understand the data -> central tendency, variation, and spread 

- Data disperion characteristics: median, max, min, quantities, outliers, variance, etc. 

- Numerical diensions correspond to sorted intervals 
    - Data dispersion: analyzed with multiple granularities of precision 
    - Boxplot or quantile analysis on computed measures 

- Dispersion analysis on computed measures 
    - Folding measures into numerical dimensions 
    - Boxplot or quantile analysis on the transformed cube 

// Measuring the Central Tedency 

- Mean (AVERAGE)
    - Sum of the values, divide by the total number of values 

- Median (EXACT MIDDLE)
    - Middle value if odd number of values, or average of the two middle values otherwise

- Mode 
    - Value that occurs most frequently in the data 


// Measuring Data Dispersion 

- Quartiles, outliers, and boxplots 
    - Quartiles: Q1 (25%), Median/Q2 (50%), Q3 (75%)
    - Interquartile range: IQR = Q3 - Q1 
    - Five number summary: min, Q1, median, Q3, MAX 
    - Boxplot: ends of the box are the quartiles, median is marked, add whiskers and plot outliers individually 
        - Outlier: usually a value higher/lower than 1.5 * IQR 

- Variance and Standard deviation 
    - Standard deviation measures how spread apart our data is 
        - Small standard deviation means less spread out data 
        - High standard deviation means more spread out distribution 
    - Variance = (standard deviation)^2

